**Core Principle Identification**: The mean molecular speed in an ideal gas is derived from the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution and requires the formula $ v = \sqrt{\frac{8RT}{\pi M}} $, where $ R = 8.314 \, \text{J/(mol·K)} $ (gas constant), $ T $ is temperature in Kelvin, and $ M $ is molar mass in **kg/mol**. This formula specifically calculates the *mean* speed (not RMS or most probable speed), and unit consistency (kg/mol for $ M $) is critical for correct SI unit output (m/s).  
**Pitfall Prediction**:  
- *Conceptual Trap*: Confusing mean speed ($ \sqrt{8RT/(\pi M)} $) with RMS speed ($ \sqrt{3RT/M} $), which would overestimate $ v $ by ~15%.  
- *Calculation Trap*: Using molar mass in g/mol (222 g/mol for Rn) without converting to kg/mol (0.222 kg/mol), causing a 31.6× error (since $ \sqrt{1000} \approx 31.6 $).  
- *Completeness Trap*: Assuming $ T = 273 \, \text{K} $ (STP) instead of standard room temperature ($ T = 293 \, \text{K} $), which would yield ~161 m/s (vs. 167 m/s).  
**Strategy Formulation**:  
1. Convert radon’s molar mass from g/mol (222 g/mol) to kg/mol (0.222 kg/mol).  
